From 0b166c37b41c722ca6974f52e02e4df5b046e4c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 10:37:51 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] daemon: make the serving retry budget a real bound

Two problems, one fix.

`RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY` was 10s against a default `jsonrpc_timeout` of 15s, which made
it a no-retry policy without looking like one. `retry_rpc` checks its deadline only
after an attempt returns, so the budget was always spent before the first attempt
could fail, and every failure gave up immediately. That is why a wallet with history
below the prune height failed outright rather than waiting. Underneath it, electrs
stopped waiting at 15s while the proxy keeps working on the block until its own
`peer_timeout`, 30s by default, and caches what it fetches -- so the fetch being
abandoned was often one the proxy went on to complete.

Raise it to 60s, which spans several attempts and reaches past that 30s, so a later
attempt picks up the block an earlier one gave up on. A proxy that also collapses
concurrent requests for the same block turns that into a join rather than a second
fetch: an improvement, not a requirement.

But a budget raised that way does not bound what it claims to. One run of blocks is
several RPCs -- read the prune height, resolve every height, fetch each block -- and
each retried on its own fresh budget. Against a source that is entirely down, and on
a pruned node one process answers all three, 60s became three 60s waits in series.
That is most of the way back to the five-minute freeze the two budgets were split up
to prevent, and it would have been a regression on the 10s rather than a fix.

So how the allowance is spent is now explicit. Serving takes one deadline for the
whole run: a failing run gives up once, not once per stage. Indexing keeps a fresh
budget per call, because there the allowance exists to ride out a restart of the
block source rather than to bound a wait, and a 2000-block batch over Tor takes
longer than any sane budget -- charging successful fetches against it would leave the
last blocks in a batch unable to survive a blip the first ones would have.

Sharing the deadline costs nothing on the serving path. It is only ever read after a
call has already failed, so a slow but working query still runs to completion however
long it takes.

What waiting costs is stated in the comment rather than waved away. The preceding
commit makes a batch cost its slowest client instead of the sum, but `handle_events`
still runs inline on the loop that drives `rpc.sync()`, so raising this raises the
worst-case indexing stall with it. Deliberate: the stall is bounded, nothing is lost
because indexing resumes where it was, and it only happens when fetches are genuinely
failing -- against which the 10s bought a wallet that could not load its history.
---
 src/daemon.rs | 168 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 153 insertions(+), 15 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/daemon.rs b/src/daemon.rs
index d6159c5..f970559 100644
--- a/src/daemon.rs
+++ b/src/daemon.rs
@@ -375,12 +375,73 @@ pub struct Daemon {
 /// riding out a real misconfiguration — past this, failing loudly is more useful
 /// than stalling silently, and the index on disk is still valid to resume from.
 const RETRY_BUDGET_INDEXING: Duration = Duration::from_secs(300);
-/// Serving a client cannot. `handle_events` and `rpc.sync()` share one thread
-/// (`server.rs`), so a query that waits also freezes indexing and every other
-/// client. A prompt error the wallet can retry beats a five-minute freeze.
-const RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(10);
+/// Serving a client can afford to wait, but less.
+///
+/// This used to be 10s, and 10s was below `jsonrpc_timeout` (15s by default), which made it
+/// silently a no-retry policy: `retry_rpc` checks its deadline only after an attempt returns, so
+/// the budget was always spent before the first attempt could fail and the deadline check fired
+/// on the first error every time. That is what made a wallet with history below the prune height
+/// fail outright rather than wait.
+///
+/// **What waiting still costs.** `handle_events` runs inline on the `server.rs` loop that also
+/// drives `rpc.sync()`. Answering the clients within one batch is parallel, so a batch costs its
+/// slowest client rather than the sum of them, but the loop is still held for that long: a slow
+/// query stalls indexing, and a request arriving mid-batch waits for the batch to end. Raising
+/// this from 10s to 60s therefore raises the worst-case stall with it. Taking the work off that
+/// loop entirely is the fix, and it is not done here.
+///
+/// That trade is deliberate. The stall is bounded, costs nothing permanent -- indexing resumes
+/// from where it was -- and only happens when fetches are actually failing, whereas the 10s
+/// bought a wallet that could not load its history at all.
+///
+/// Sized against what is on the other end. A single attempt is capped by `jsonrpc_timeout`, but
+/// the proxy in front of a pruned node keeps working on the block for its own `peer_timeout` (30s
+/// by default) after we have stopped waiting, and it caches what it fetches. So the budget is set
+/// to span several attempts and reach past that 30s: a later attempt picks up the block the
+/// earlier one gave up on. A proxy that also collapses concurrent requests for the same block
+/// makes that a join rather than a second fetch, which is a further improvement and not a
+/// requirement.
+///
+/// Spent as one allowance for the whole run rather than per call -- see [`Retry`]. A run reads the
+/// prune height, resolves heights, and fetches each block, and giving each of those its own fresh
+/// 60s would mean a source that is entirely down costs three waits in series rather than one.
+const RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(60);
 const RPC_RETRY_MAX_DELAY: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);
 
+/// How the allowance above is spent across the several RPCs one run of blocks needs.
+///
+/// A run is not one call. It reads the prune height, resolves every block's height, and then
+/// fetches each block, and each of those retries on its own. Giving all of them the same fresh
+/// budget multiplies it: against a source that is entirely down -- which on a pruned node is one
+/// process answering all three -- a 60s allowance becomes three 60s waits in series. That is
+/// most of the way back to the five-minute freeze the two budgets were split up to prevent.
+#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
+enum Retry {
+    /// One deadline covering the whole run.
+    ///
+    /// For serving, where what is being bounded is how long the `server.rs` loop is held. A run
+    /// that is failing gives up once, not once per stage. Time spent succeeding counts against it,
+    /// which costs nothing: the deadline is only ever read after a call has already failed, so a
+    /// slow but working query runs to completion however long it takes.
+    WholeRun { deadline: Instant },
+    /// A fresh budget for each call.
+    ///
+    /// For indexing, where the allowance exists to ride out a restart of the block source rather
+    /// than to bound a wait. A batch of 2000 blocks over Tor takes longer than any sane budget, so
+    /// charging successful fetches against it would mean the last blocks in a batch could not
+    /// survive a blip the first ones would have.
+    PerCall { budget: Duration },
+}
+
+impl Retry {
+    fn deadline(self) -> Instant {
+        match self {
+            Retry::WholeRun { deadline } => deadline,
+            Retry::PerCall { budget } => Instant::now() + budget,
+        }
+    }
+}
+
 /// How many RPC connections to the node to keep.
 ///
 /// Each one is a socket, and each is a slot for work that would otherwise queue behind whatever
@@ -533,10 +594,10 @@ impl Daemon {
     fn retry_rpc<T>(
         &self,
         what: &str,
-        budget: Duration,
+        deadline: Instant,
         mut f: impl FnMut() -> Result<T>,
     ) -> Result<T> {
-        let deadline = Instant::now() + budget;
+        let started = Instant::now();
         let mut delay = Duration::from_secs(1);
         let mut attempt = 0usize;
         loop {
@@ -553,7 +614,7 @@ impl Daemon {
                             format!(
                                 "{} still failing after {}s of retries",
                                 what,
-                                budget.as_secs()
+                                started.elapsed().as_secs()
                             )
                         });
                     }
@@ -743,7 +804,13 @@ impl Daemon {
         B: IntoIterator<Item = BlockHash>,
         F: FnMut(BlockHash, SerBlock),
     {
-        self.for_blocks_with(blockhashes, func, RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY)
+        self.for_blocks_with(
+            blockhashes,
+            func,
+            Retry::WholeRun {
+                deadline: Instant::now() + RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY,
+            },
+        )
     }
 
     /// Blocks for building the index. Retries patiently — see
@@ -753,10 +820,16 @@ impl Daemon {
         B: IntoIterator<Item = BlockHash>,
         F: FnMut(BlockHash, SerBlock),
     {
-        self.for_blocks_with(blockhashes, func, RETRY_BUDGET_INDEXING)
+        self.for_blocks_with(
+            blockhashes,
+            func,
+            Retry::PerCall {
+                budget: RETRY_BUDGET_INDEXING,
+            },
+        )
     }
 
-    fn for_blocks_with<B, F>(&self, blockhashes: B, mut func: F, budget: Duration) -> Result<()>
+    fn for_blocks_with<B, F>(&self, blockhashes: B, mut func: F, retry: Retry) -> Result<()>
     where
         B: IntoIterator<Item = BlockHash>,
         F: FnMut(BlockHash, SerBlock),
@@ -781,8 +854,8 @@ impl Daemon {
             return Ok(());
         }
         let prune_height =
-            self.retry_rpc("reading prune height", budget, || self.prune_height())?;
-        let heights = self.retry_rpc("resolving block heights", budget, || {
+            self.retry_rpc("reading prune height", retry.deadline(), || self.prune_height())?;
+        let heights = self.retry_rpc("resolving block heights", retry.deadline(), || {
             self.block_heights(&blockhashes)
         })?;
 
@@ -822,7 +895,7 @@ impl Daemon {
                 pipeline(
                     run,
                     FETCH_CONCURRENCY,
-                    |i, &hash| self.fetch_pruned_block(hash, heights[start + i], budget),
+                    |i, &hash| self.fetch_pruned_block(hash, heights[start + i], retry),
                     |&hash, block| {
                         func(hash, block);
                         Ok(())
@@ -854,12 +927,12 @@ impl Daemon {
         &self,
         hash: BlockHash,
         height: usize,
-        budget: Duration,
+        retry: Retry,
     ) -> Result<SerBlock> {
         if let Some(block) = self.block_from_helper(hash, height) {
             return Ok(block);
         }
-        self.retry_rpc(&format!("fetching pruned block {}", hash), budget, || {
+        self.retry_rpc(&format!("fetching pruned block {}", hash), retry.deadline(), || {
             self.get_block_raw_rpc(hash)
         })
         .with_context(|| {
@@ -1042,6 +1115,71 @@ mod tests {
         assert_eq!(info.prune_height, None);
     }
 
+    /// Serving spends one allowance across the whole run; indexing gets a fresh one per call.
+    ///
+    /// The distinction is the whole reason `Retry` exists. A run is several RPCs, so a per-call
+    /// budget on the serving path multiplies into that many waits in series against a source that
+    /// is down, which is what the serving budget is there to bound.
+    #[test]
+    fn serving_shares_one_deadline_while_indexing_renews_it() {
+        use std::time::{Duration, Instant};
+
+        let fixed = Instant::now() + Duration::from_secs(60);
+        let whole = super::Retry::WholeRun { deadline: fixed };
+        assert_eq!(whole.deadline(), fixed, "the run shares one deadline");
+        std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(10));
+        assert_eq!(whole.deadline(), fixed, "and it does not move as the run goes on");
+
+        let per_call = super::Retry::PerCall {
+            budget: Duration::from_secs(300),
+        };
+        let first = per_call.deadline();
+        std::thread::sleep(Duration::from_millis(10));
+        assert!(
+            per_call.deadline() > first,
+            "each indexing call starts its allowance afresh"
+        );
+    }
+
+    /// The budget has to outlast a failed attempt, or it is a no-retry policy wearing a retry's
+    /// clothes: `retry_rpc` checks the deadline only after an attempt returns, so a budget
+    /// shorter than one attempt gives up on the first error every time. That is what 10s did.
+    #[test]
+    fn the_query_budget_outlasts_a_stalled_fetch() {
+        use std::time::Duration;
+
+        // Both defaults live outside this file: `jsonrpc_timeout_secs` is 15 in
+        // internal/config_specification.toml, and btc-rpc-proxy's `peer_timeout` is 30 in its
+        // config_spec.toml. Named here so putting the budget back under either fails loudly.
+        const ATTEMPT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(15);
+        const PROXY_PEER_TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(30);
+
+        let budget = super::RETRY_BUDGET_QUERY;
+        assert!(budget > ATTEMPT, "a budget under one attempt never retries");
+
+        // Walk the loop `retry_rpc` runs: attempt, check the deadline, back off, repeat.
+        let mut elapsed = Duration::ZERO;
+        let mut delay = Duration::from_secs(1);
+        let mut attempts = 0;
+        loop {
+            elapsed += ATTEMPT;
+            attempts += 1;
+            if elapsed >= budget {
+                break;
+            }
+            elapsed += delay;
+            delay *= 2;
+        }
+
+        assert!(attempts >= 2, "only {} attempt(s) fit the budget", attempts);
+        assert!(
+            elapsed > PROXY_PEER_TIMEOUT,
+            "gave up after {:?}, before the proxy stops working on the block at {:?}",
+            elapsed,
+            PROXY_PEER_TIMEOUT
+        );
+    }
+
     /// Delivery follows the request order whatever order the fetches finish in, and no more
     /// than `concurrency` run at once. Both matter: callers accumulate state through `deliver`,
     /// and the bound is what keeps a run of thousands of megabyte blocks from being held at once.
